A||||3||JetroGT||Loud Rear Wheel Noise||||1||
Z||000000||JetroGT||03-20-2000||02:20 PM||||HELP!<BR>I have a loud noise only when the wheels are turning. I just bought new tires because of this and the noise is still there. Could it be berrings, CV joints, or bad rear end alingment?<BR>I just had the berrings replaced not too long ago. Along with rotors pads and calipers. <BR>I could describe this noise as a, "Whoomp" every time the wheel rotates. And it starts up at 15 Mph then gets louder.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-20-2000||03:30 PM||||Bad CV joints on a fiero have been described as making a rumbling noise at normal driving speed. Often there is a vibration when accelerating. I'd jack up the rear, pull the tires off and spin the rotors to see if they make noise. It might be something simple like a rotor scraping against a bent brake dust shield or maybe the bearings were not install correctly, or a large number of other problems. Track down the noise. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JetroGT||03-20-2000||04:00 PM||||I just got back from the body shop where my blue GT is getting a nose job. I asked the owner what he thought about the noise. "Warped roter" Makes sense. When I stomp on the brake the noise almost totally goes away. I'm taking the car back to the shop where I had the rear brakes done. Hopefully at no charge.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-20-2000||05:27 PM||||A warped rotor will usually cause a slight pulsing sensation in the brake pedal that you can feel during braking. Maybe the rear rotors aren't as noticeable as the front ones.||||reg||1
